= Introduction<intro>

== Motivation
  
Artificial intelligence and respectively deep learning architectures are currently beeing developed quite fast this time and optimized more and more. With the introduction of transformer models user-oriented applications to generate text, audio and videos or identify everdays sequences featuring trainable logic. Furthermore they are declared as all-rounder tools, capeable to slowly making the first jobs obsolete which shows the question if they are really able to work as a multi-tool or if they just take profit from the large range of training data and if there will be some kind of bias due to coarse data with less depth and no focus on a special task like emotion detection. 

From an early stage psychological approaches are showing fixed behavioral patterns when showing emotions and sentiments including face and voice actions regarding semantic and syntax. Basic math and machine learning algorithms were able to classify some information and identify emotion thus neural networks are bringing more performance, better results in less time. The introduced transformers are infamous for their contextual understanding as well as very good and consistent outcomes.

This leads to the question if already developed applications like ChatGPT or in general approaches based on deep learning are able to recognize emotion from video, audio and text in the context of static interviews and real-life excerpts. The goal of this thesis is to compare state of the art models and architectures mostly focused on transformers which are present in every modal. Further multimodal emotion detection might be a topic to analyize different feature fusion models and if the differnt labels from the different architectures might lead to an more accurate result than a single modal outcome.  This topic leads from basic voting strategies up to further more complex architectures which are using the logits, kernels or other raw output from the models. A comparison can be done to show if the effort is necessary for an accurate result or if given basic methods are nearly as good or even better. These points sequels into an assumption that bimodal emotion classifcation can be more efficient and asymptotically accurate as well as trimodal classification which might show that one of the modals might have a small impact on the results but not an crucial one. Intuitively this can be emotion classification with features from text due to the impact of irony or other vocal features bringing a not unambigious meaning for sentences or words related to their emotion label. The opportunity to ignore one or more modals offers several optimizations as for example one large architecture less is needed or the choosen feature combination method has a reduction in complexity and computation time. 

In general emotion detection brings an interesting field of opportunity for research regarding machine-based simulation of human behavior and understanding of interactions related to interviews or conversations. Also after learning from the features most generative models are able to reproduce emotion along the trained information giving perspective in robotics or other forms of real world imitations. Current research still focused on the general, non-transformer based models like convolution, reccurent networks or even simpler machine learning approaches. Due to limitation from copyright or closed-source projects a few architectures are reproduced but aren't trained one the original datasets which might lead to different accuracys in comparison to the usage of the original model with the pretrained weights. 


== Structure

This work starts by showing and discussing the fundamental, psychological backgrounds in Chapter 2 for the term emotion and which features are necessary to identify them. Further the classifcation approaches in machine and deep learning are presented showing their unsupervised and supervised methods for training and feature extraction. At the end of this chapter the datasets for the further implementation parts are introduced. Related works in Chapter 3 are presented to display current research on multi-modal detection and their different methods and learning proceedings. Continuing with the design and implementation of the different supervised models, Chapter 4 presents self-trained and also pretrained models and how to expand as well as finetune their knowledge. In addition the architectures to fuse the information for multi-model emotion detection are shown and different fusion methods are discussed including some kinds of basic scoring and more advanced feature combinations. These accomplishments are evaluated in Chapter 5 introducting measurement criteria for relieable and comparable results. At the end this thesis is summarized and gives an overwiev in comparison to some outcomes from related work concluding with a look on possible future work in emotion detection. 


